::ENG4_U02_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: By morning, the city wore a coat of broken branches. Rainwater whispered along the curb, carrying leaves like small, exhausted boats. In the park, the oldest oak lifted one split limb toward the pale sky, not in defeat but in accusation. People stepped around the puddles quietly, as if the storm had taken the street's voice and left everyone listening for what it would say next. What is the effect of "the city wore a coat of broken branches"? {
=A. It makes storm damage seem like a heavy covering on the city.#Correct. The metaphor makes damage visible and burdensome.
~B. It says the city bought clothing.#This is too literal.
~C. It proves the branches were clean.#This is not stated.
~D. It removes the storm's impact.#It emphasizes the impact.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: Rainwater whispered along the curb. What does whispered imply? {
~A. Quiet movement#Correct. The word creates a hushed sound.
~B. Public shouting#This is the opposite.
~C. Bright color#This is unrelated.
~D. Heavy construction#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: carrying leaves like small, exhausted boats. Which device is used? {
~A. Hyperbole only#The comparison uses like.
~B. Simile#Correct. The phrase compares leaves to boats using like.
~C. Allusion#No outside reference is made.
~D. Direct address#No one is addressed.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: leaves like small, exhausted boats. What is the figurative effect? {
~A. It makes leaves seem weary and adrift after the storm.#Correct. The simile shapes tone.
~B. It proves leaves are human-made boats.#This is literal misreading.
~C. It creates a cheerful celebration.#The word exhausted creates weariness.
~D. It explains boat construction.#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: the oldest oak lifted one split limb toward the pale sky. What is being personified? {
~A. The oak#Correct. The tree is described as lifting a limb like a person.
~B. The morning#The phrase focuses on the oak.
~C. The curb#The curb is not personified here.
~D. The puddles#Puddles appear later.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: not in defeat but in accusation. What does accusation suggest? {
~A. Blame or charge of wrongdoing#Correct. The word suggests judgment.
~B. Complete peace#This is the opposite.
~C. A small boat#This is unrelated.
~D. A weather report#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: the oldest oak lifted one split limb toward the pale sky, not in defeat but in accusation. Which evaluation is best? {
~A. The personification gives the damaged tree moral force, as if it responds to destruction.#Correct. This explains effect.
~B. The phrase gives a scientific definition of oak trees.#It is figurative.
~C. The phrase makes the scene funny.#The tone is serious.
~D. The phrase means the tree is not damaged.#It has a split limb.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: as if the storm had taken the street's voice. What does this phrase mean? {
~A. The storm created an unnatural silence.#Correct. The street is personified as having a voice.
~B. The storm taught the street to sing.#This is too literal.
~C. The street wrote a speech.#This is too literal.
~D. The city became louder than before.#The phrase suggests quiet.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Passage: By morning, the city wore a coat of broken branches. Rainwater whispered along the curb. If read alongside poetry traditions that use nature imagery to express emotion, what does the passage do? {
~A. It uses natural damage to express a human sense of aftermath.#Correct. This connects imagery and emotion.
~B. It avoids emotional meaning.#The imagery creates mood.
~C. It gives only a weather report.#The language is literary.
~D. It rejects all poetic technique.#It uses several techniques.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: pale sky. What connotation does pale most likely add? {
~A. Weakness or drained color#Correct. It supports the subdued mood.
~B. Loud celebration#This does not fit.
~C. Mechanical speed#This is unrelated.
~D. Deep anger only#Pale suggests drained or faint.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: People stepped around the puddles quietly, as if the storm had taken the street's voice. What effect does the figurative language have on tone? {
~A. It creates a hushed, reflective tone.#Correct. The street's lost voice suggests silence.
~B. It creates a playful tone.#The aftermath is serious.
~C. It creates a triumphant tone.#The passage is subdued.
~D. It creates a careless tone.#People move quietly.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: the city wore a coat of broken branches. What is the literal meaning beneath the metaphor? {
~A. Broken branches covered parts of the city.#Correct. That is the literal situation.
~B. The city bought a coat.#This is too literal.
~C. The city became a person.#This is figurative.
~D. The branches were clothing.#This is too literal.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: Rainwater whispered along the curb. Why is whispered more effective than moved? {
~A. It adds sound, mood, and personification.#Correct. The word does more than report motion.
~B. It removes imagery.#It adds imagery.
~C. It makes the line purely literal.#It is figurative.
~D. It makes the rainwater disappear.#It describes the water.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: exhausted boats. Which phrase best explains exhausted in context? {
~A. Drained and weary after forceful movement#Correct. This fits the post-storm setting.
~B. Bright and new#This is the opposite.
~C. Mathematically exact#This is unrelated.
~D. Legally accused#This belongs to another phrase.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: not in defeat but in accusation. What contrast does this phrase create? {
~A. It contrasts passive damage with active judgment.#Correct. The oak is not just broken; it seems to accuse.
~B. It contrasts water with fire.#This is not the contrast.
~C. It contrasts boats with leaves only.#That is a different image.
~D. It contrasts morning with night.#This is not central.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: left everyone listening for what it would say next. What is personified? {
~A. The silent street or storm aftermath#Correct. The passage suggests the damaged street might speak.
~B. A textbook#No textbook appears.
~C. A literal boat#The boats are a simile for leaves.
~D. A courtroom#No courtroom appears.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: The city wore a coat; rainwater whispered; the storm took the street's voice. What pattern is created by these images? {
~A. The environment is treated as if it has a human body and voice.#Correct. This pattern personifies the cityscape.
~B. The passage removes human qualities from everything.#It does the opposite.
~C. The passage becomes a set of instructions.#It remains descriptive.
~D. The imagery is unrelated.#The images create a consistent mood.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: left everyone listening for what it would say next. What does listening suggest? {
~A. The people are alert to the meaning of the aftermath.#Correct. The word suggests attentiveness.
~B. The people are ignoring damage.#This is the opposite.
~C. The people are singing loudly.#This is not stated.
~D. The people are repairing boats.#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Which response evaluates figurative language most effectively? Passage: Rainwater whispered along the curb. {
~A. The word whispered personifies rainwater and makes the aftermath feel quiet and tense.#Correct. This identifies and evaluates effect.
~B. The phrase has rainwater in it.#This only identifies topic.
~C. The water is literally talking.#This misreads the figure.
~D. The phrase is good because it is nice.#This is vague.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: carrying leaves like small, exhausted boats. How does the simile support the passage's mood? {
~A. It extends the mood of fatigue and aftermath.#Correct. Exhausted boats suggest weariness.
~B. It creates a mood of noisy celebration.#This does not fit.
~C. It makes the passage informational only.#It is figurative.
~D. It removes the storm from memory.#It reinforces storm aftermath.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] If this passage were compared with a major poet's use of nature imagery, what should the analysis focus on? {
~A. How natural images carry emotional or thematic meaning.#Correct. This connects technique and interpretation.
~B. Whether the passage mentions every type of tree.#That is not literary evaluation.
~C. Whether storms exist in real life.#That is not the focus.
~D. Whether the passage is a map.#It is not.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: People stepped around the puddles quietly. How does this literal detail support the figurative language around it? {
~A. It grounds the personification in a real post-storm scene.#Correct. Literal detail supports figurative effect.
~B. It proves there is no figurative language.#Figurative language surrounds it.
~C. It makes the city joyful.#The detail is subdued.
~D. It explains how to drain streets.#This is not the purpose.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: small, exhausted boats. What does small add to the image? {
~A. Fragility#Correct. Small adds delicacy and vulnerability.
~B. Enormous power#This is the opposite.
~C. Legal authority#This is unrelated.
~D. Loud sound#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] Passage: The oldest oak lifted one split limb toward the pale sky. What is the effect of limb? {
~A. It makes the tree seem body-like and wounded.#Correct. Limb can refer to a branch or body part.
~B. It makes the tree mechanical.#This is not suggested.
~C. It removes personification.#It supports personification.
~D. It means the tree is a boat.#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.09] What is the best overall evaluation of the author's figurative language? Passage: By morning, the city wore a coat of broken branches. Rainwater whispered along the curb, carrying leaves like small, exhausted boats. In the park, the oldest oak lifted one split limb toward the pale sky, not in defeat but in accusation. People stepped around the puddles quietly, as if the storm had taken the street's voice and left everyone listening for what it would say next. {
~A. The figurative language turns storm damage into a scene of exhaustion, silence, and judgment.#Correct. This evaluates the pattern across the passage.
~B. The figurative language proves the storm was imaginary.#This is false.
~C. The figurative language only decorates the passage without adding meaning.#It shapes meaning and tone.
~D. The figurative language makes the passage a comedy.#The tone is not comic.
}
